UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 6: Food safety and hygiene', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C. In that range many bacteria can multiply if food stays there long enough. Cold food belongs in the fridge, at or below 5°C. Hot food is kept hot, at or above 60°C, or cooled quickly and refrigerated. Time matters as well as temperature: a rice dish left on a warm bench all afternoon is a different risk from the same dish served straight away.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA meat pie sits in a school bag from 8 am until 1 pm. Is “it was wrapped” a control for the danger zone? What should happen to the pie?\n\nFEEDBACK\nWrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot. It likely sat in the danger zone for hours. Do not eat it. Next time use an ice brick in an insulated bag or choose a shelf-stable lunch.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe. Some toxins are not destroyed by a later boil, and the time already passed still counts.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSort these four foods into “serve now or chill” versus “time in the danger zone is already too long”: soup just off the stove; soup on the bench for four hours; yoghurt from the fridge; a sandwich in the sun for three hours. Explain one choice.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C. In that range many bacteria can multiply if food stays there long enough. Cold food belongs in the fridge, at or below 5°C. Hot food is kept hot, at or above 60°C, or cooled quickly and refrigerated. Time matters as well as temperature: a rice dish left on a warm bench all afternoon is a different risk from the same dish served straight away.","lesson_number":6,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-SAF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Sort these four foods into “serve now or chill” versus “time in the danger zone is already too long”: soup just off the stove; soup on the bench for four hours; yoghurt from the fridge; a sandwich in the sun for three ho Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using danger zone on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe.. Correction: Wrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe. Some toxins are not destroyed by a later boil, and the time already passed still counts. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating danger zone on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with danger zone, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about danger zone with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for danger zone. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for danger zone. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Wrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot","Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work"],"question":"A meat pie sits in a school bag from 8 am until 1 pm. Is “it was wrapped” a control for the danger zone? What should happen to the pie?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for danger zone; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Wrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot","Careful use of temperature with clear evidence","Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe","The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with danger zone?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named temperature, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name temperature only, without linking it to danger zone","Apply danger zone to new material and name the temperature used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing that reheating always 