a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for danger zone?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sort these four foods into “serve now or chill” versus “time in the danger zone is already too long”: soup just off the","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe","Name temperature only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies danger zone to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about danger zone with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating danger zone on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using danger zone on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for danger zone. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with danger zone, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe.. Correction: Wrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w03-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) says Stage 4 teachers may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). This lesson uses FT5-SAF-01 as a pending proposal. The 2019 syllabus remains in teach-out. Not NESA verified. Practical cooking is optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise danger zone.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use danger zone with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify temperature and explain why it matters.","I can practise time independently on a new prompt.","I can notice chill needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about danger zone?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCooked rice at 1:00 pm is still on the stove, lukewarm, at 5:00 pm. It has spent hours in the danger zone. The safe decision is to discard it, not to reheat it and hope. A contrast: rice packed into the fridge within an hour in a shallow container spends little time in that range.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA meat pie sits in a school bag from 8 am until 1 pm. Is “it was wrapped” a control for the danger zone? What should happen to the pie?\n\nFEEDBACK\nWrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot. It likely sat in the danger zone for hours. Do not eat it. Next time use an ice brick in an insulated bag or choose a shelf-stable lunch.","explain":"The temperature danger zone used in Australian food-safety teaching is about 5°C to 60°C. In that range many bacteria can multiply if food stays there long enough. Cold food belongs in the fridge, at or below 5°C. Hot food is kept hot, at or above 60°C, or cooled quickly and refrigerated. Time matters as well as temperature: a rice dish left on a warm bench all afternoon is a different risk from the same dish served straight away.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe. Some toxins are not destroyed by a later boil, and the time already passed still counts. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"The danger zone is about 5°C to 60°C; cold or hot food leaves that range.","caption":"Temperature and time both count.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Danger zone</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">about 5°C to 60°C</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safe place</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fridge or steaming hot</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Cooked rice at 1:00 pm is still on the stove, lukewarm, at 5:00 pm. It has spent hours in the danger zone. The safe deci Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Cooked rice at 1:00 pm is still on the stove, lukewarm, at 5:00 pm. It has spent hours in the danger zone. The safe deci Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about danger zone?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCooked rice at 1:00 pm is still on the stove, lukewarm, at 5:00 pm. It has spent hours in the danger zone. The safe decision is to discard it, not to reheat it and hope. A contrast: rice packed into the fridge within an hour in a shallow container spends little time in that range. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Rice left warm for hours is discarded; quickly chilled rice is the contrast.","caption":"Reheating is not a repair for a long warm hold.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Rice 4 hours warm</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">discard</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rice chilled fast</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">shallow, fridge</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about danger zone?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that reheating always makes danger-zone food safe. Some toxins are not destroyed by a later boil, and the time already passed still counts. Here is a more productive model: Cooked rice at 1:00 pm is still on the stove, lukewarm, at 5:00 pm. It has spent hours in the danger zone. The safe decision is to discard it, not to reheat it and hope. A contrast: rice packed into the fridge within an hour in a shallow container spends little time in that range.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nWrapping does not keep the pie cold or hot. It likely sat in the danger zone for hours. Do not eat it. Next time use an ice brick in an insulated bag or choose a shelf-stable lunch.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO 